{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792348343,"nonce":"TMXvc+YlY6FYrlTAps4HOqcA5g8=","title":"A var() in a gradient's colour stop raises a null access while styling","message":"**What happens**\n\n`background: linear-gradient(150deg, var(--glass-a), var(--glass-b));` on a card, with `--glass-a` and `--glass-b` set on an ancestor (`.dash-root { --glass-a: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.66); … }`, redefined inside `@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark)`), stops the program while the tree is styled:\n\n    Exception: Null access\n    Called from ashui.css.$CssValue.color (ashui/css/CssValue.hx line 417)\n    Called from ashui.css.$CssValue.stops (ashui/css/CssValue.hx line 701)\n    Called from ashui.css.$CssValue.gradient (ashui/css/CssValue.hx line 614)\n    Called from ashui.css.$Properties.image (ashui/css/Properties.hx line 1467)\n\n`background: var(--glass-tile)` with the same kind of property is fine, and so are literal colours in the same gradient.\n\n**Round it**\n\napps/dashboard writes the stops as literals, once for each scheme.\n\n**To find out**\n\nWhether the cause is the var() not being replaced before the stops are read, or the custom property being found only when the element is in a scheme the sheet's media query matches; the app's reproducer is apps/dashboard/src/dashboard/dashboard.css before the commit that introduced the glass.","files":null}]}